Finding: {"title":"findCustomerByName uses non-functional escaping for QBO query strings — malformed queries for names containing apostrophes","severity":"MEDIUM","area":"business-rules / correctness","files":["src/lib/quickbooks.ts"],"detail":"`findCustomerByName` (src/lib/quickbooks.ts:343-348) escapes single quotes with `name.replace(/'/g, \"\\\\'\")` (backslash-escape) before embedding into a QBO SQL-like query string: `SELECT * FROM Customer WHERE DisplayName = '${escaped}'`. QuickBooks Online's query language does not support backslash-escaping of quotes — it follows standard SQL convention where a literal single quote is escaped by doubling it (`''`). A sibling QBO integration in the same codebase, `src/server/assistant/tools/native/qbo.ts` (line 140-141, `escapeQboString`), correctly doubles the quote and is verified by a test asserting `DisplayName = 'O''Hare'`.","impact":"Any flight school whose name contains an apostrophe (e.g. \"O'Brien Aviation\") will send a malformed query to the QBO API, throwing a query-parse error and breaking `findOrCreateQBCustomer` → invoice sync fails for that school.","recommendation":"Replace `name.replace(/'/g, \"\\\\'\")` with `name.replace(/'/g, \"''\")`, or extract a shared `escapeQboString` helper reused by both `src/lib/quickbooks.ts` and `src/server/assistant/tools/native/qbo.ts`. Add a unit test mirroring the existing `qbo.test.ts` apostrophe case.","confidence":"HIGH"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

